# A lower layer does not know what is built on it

## Why this matters

Composer keeps its lower layers small and general, and builds everything
specific on top of them. Core models services, resources and connections.
RPC is one kind of connection, built in `service-rpc`. Prisma Cloud is one
deployment target, built in its own packages. Cron and streams are modules
built on both.

That arrangement is what lets someone add a connection kind, a target or a
module without changing core, and lets a target implement only what every
service needs.

Each specific concept written into a lower layer undoes a little of that:

- Every implementer of the interface now has to provide it, including targets
  and services that have no such thing.
- The lower layer has to change whenever the higher one does.
- The next connection kind or module has an equal claim to a member of its
  own.

The dependency check does not catch this. It looks at imports, and a concept
can be written into an interface without importing anything.

## When it comes up

It rarely looks like a decision about layers. It looks like a shortcut. Code in
a higher layer needs a value. The only thing it holds is an interface from a
lower layer. Adding one member to that interface is the shortest way to get the
value. That is the moment to stop and ask who the member is for.

## How to tell

- **Would every implementer have a real answer?** Every service has
  dependencies to load and an input to read. Only a service that serves RPC has
  accepted RPC keys.
- **Who would call it?** If the only caller is one package in a higher layer,
  the member serves that package's concept.
- **Does a more general name change either answer?** It does not.
  `acceptedKeys()` or `reservedValue(name)` in place of `acceptedRpcKeys()` is
  the same member with the concept hidden.

A member does belong in a lower layer when that layer's own model needs it:
something true of every service, every resource or every target. This rule is
not a reason to avoid such a change.

## What to do

Make the change in the layer that owns the concept. Often the higher layer can
ask for what it needs through something the lower layer already offers to
everyone, such as a declared parameter, a field of the service's input, or a
dependency's binding, so the value arrives the way any other value does.

If nothing fits, you have found a gap in the design, and that is worth
reporting as it is: what the higher layer needs, and why nothing that exists
carries it.

Do not present the lower-layer change as one of the options, whether the idea
is yours or a subagent's. The person deciding would have to notice the problem
themselves, and the gap you found goes unreported.

## Example

The RPC request handler `serve()`, in `service-rpc`, needed the service keys it
accepts. It held only core's service interface. An agent proposed that the
interface gain a method returning the accepted RPC keys, and asked the operator
to approve it. Every target's service would then have had to implement a method
that exists for one kind of connection. The operator rejected it.

The principles behind this are "Compose, don't special-case" and "Thin core,
fat targets" in `docs/design/01-principles/guiding-principles.md`.
`docs/design/03-domain-model/core-and-targets.md` describes the split.
